Side note- The menu online has slightly different descriptions than what we had, so perhaps it changes a bit seasonally.

I was at this Israeli restaurant a few months back with two others, having also gone to their more casual Miriam Street Food location in Brooklyn before. As usual with those two, we overordered, leaving us with leftovers, with the server happily obliging. I don't think I had anything to drink besides maybe a glass of wine, but I might be mistaken (I definitely didn't have a cocktail.)

We started with a handful of appetizers and a salad. The Hummus for $16, with tadka-spiced wild mushrooms, harissa, and pita, was pretty solid. There were plenty of mushrooms, and the hummus had a good texture, being slightly thick but not too much so, and the pita was standard. The harissa on the side of the plate was a unique touch, adding some spiciness if desired, and reminded me a bit of having shifka or another type of spicy pickle with hummus or other dips. It was good overall, though I think the hummus is a bit better quality at a few other spots on the UWS.

The Eggplant for $15, with tomato confit, melted feta, halloumi, mozzarella, and green tahini, was more unique. The dish was quite rich overall, and a bit salty, and the eggplant itself had a soft texture and was well-roasted. I would recommend it as a mezze, but only for sharing, as I think it will get a bit too rich eventually.

The Halloumi Salad, for $23, with baby arugula, grilled artichoke, walnuts, string beans, cremini mushrooms, and walnut vinaigrette, was pretty good. The cheese was nice along with the mix of vegetables, but I found the dish overall a bit too sour and salty between the vinaigrette and the cheese. I think a slightly sweeter dressing would be more preferable. I think others might also find it a bit wet for a salad, but I didn't mind.

My main is the Steak Levant, for $34, which is listed online now with tri-tip steak, Swiss chard, white and fava bean stew, root vegetable purée, and Jerusalem artichoke chips. Back then, or now, if the menu wasn't updated, it was simply served with roasted tomatoes and shoestring fries. The steak was pretty solid and quite tender, and while it lacked a bit of salt, the fries helped. The fries themselves were a mix of textures, with some pretty crispy and some a bit too soggy, though that was perhaps because some were in the meat or tomato juice. It was pretty good overall, though a bit one-note with a sauce likely helping with the fries.

Another person got the Lamb Shawarma Terracotta, for the same price with majadra rice, slow-roasted tomato, harissa, yogurt sauce, and almonds. I tried a bit of this, and it reminded me of a better version of the same dish at either Acadia or Barbounia. At those spots, the yogurt is a bit too strong, and the meat just isn't as good as it is less flavored. For this spot, the lamb was cooked more properly, and the whole dish had a bit more balance with the harissa adding a nice extra kick of spice. This was good, and I preferred it over my main. I believe the other person got the same steak as I.

We shared a dessert, which was easily the weakest thing we had. This was the Rose Babka for $15, served with vanilla ice cream. I expected it to have a rosewater flavor, given that the spot is Israeli, but in fact, the babka just really resembled one. I wouldn't have minded if the flavor was good, but the babka itself was very dry, and even though it looked like there was a good amount of chocolate, it was pretty lightly flavored. Outside of the alright ice cream, this was honestly terrible and the weakest dessert of any similar Levant spot in the area, with all three of us not wanting to finish it.

This was overall a good spot, though I have preferred some others. It wasn't the best for any specific dish, but it was solid overall.
